IMPORTANT JENKINSFILE DETAILS
-----------------------------
The tools block should refer to the names configured in Jenkins:
tools {
    jdk 'JDK21'
    maven 'M3'
}

The SonarQube server name must match:
withSonarQubeEnv('SonarCloud')

Use the following Maven command pattern inside the project folder:
mvn sonar:sonar   -Dsonar.projectKey=Samina-05_sonar-qube-java-project   -Dsonar.organization=samina-05   -Dsonar.host.url=https://sonarcloud.io

The SonarCloud token should be supplied securely by Jenkins configuration.
Never hard-code it in the command or source files.

QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS / TROUBLESHOOTING
---------------------------------------
Q: Can both main and master branches exist?
A: Yes. This does not automatically cause a problem. Configure Jenkins to use the
   branch containing the current code and Jenkinsfile.

Q: Why did Jenkins say it could not find Jenkinsfile?
A: The file is in java-maven-sonarcloud-project/. Use that folder in Script Path.

Q: Why did Maven say there was no POM in the workspace?
A: Maven ran from the repository root, but pom.xml is in a subfolder. Use the
   dir('java-maven-sonarcloud-project') block around Maven commands.

Q: Why did Maven report "release version 17 not supported" or
   "release version 21 not supported"?
A: Maven was using an older or incompatible JDK. Check `mvn -version` in the
   Jenkins Console Output. Configure Jenkins to use JDK21 and verify that the
   Java home path is valid for the Jenkins agent.

Q: What should successful build output look like?
A: Maven should show BUILD SUCCESS. The unit tests should pass. SonarCloud analysis
   should complete, the Quality Gate should pass, and Jenkins should archive a JAR.
   The overall build should finish with SUCCESS. Exact log messages can vary.

Q: Why can the Quality Gate stage keep waiting?
A: Check the SonarQube Cloud webhook configuration and make sure SonarQube Cloud
   can reach Jenkins. The webhook URL generally ends in /sonarqube-webhook/.
   If Jenkins is private or only accessible locally, SonarQube Cloud may not reach it.

Q: Does the pipeline enforce an 80% Quality Gate automatically?
A: No. Configure the relevant 80% condition in SonarQube Cloud Quality Gate settings.
   Coverage on New Code and overall coverage are different metrics.

Q: Where can I see the generated JAR?
A: Open the successful Jenkins build and look in its Artifacts section. The file is
   expected under java-maven-sonarcloud-project/target/.

Q: Should I commit the SonarCloud token to GitHub?
A: No. Keep it in Jenkins Credentials as a secret text credential.

